## Lab Architecture

| Role | Operating System / Platform | Hostname | IP Address | Key Services |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Attacker VM** | Kali Linux | `kali` | `192.168.140.148` | Automated HTTP POST brute-force scripts |
| **Target VM** | Ubuntu Server | `jeanpubuntu` | `192.168.140.150` | Docker (`vigilant_hermann` / DVWA on Port 80), Wazuh Agent |
| **SIEM Server** | Ubuntu / Wazuh Manager | `wazuh-server` | `192.168.140.151` | Wazuh Manager, Indexer, Dashboard |

### Telemetry & Response Flow
1. **Attack Simulation:** Kali Linux (`192.168.140.148`) sends high-frequency automated HTTP POST requests to `http://192.168.140`.
2. **Log Generation & Decoding:** Apache logs recurring PHP notices (`Undefined index: session_token in /var/www/html/login.php`), parsed by Wazuh's `apache-errorlog` decoder under base **Rule `30318`**.
3. **Custom Detection & Correlation:** Custom rules in `/var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml` filter `login.php` anomalies (**Rule `100010`**) and correlate bursts of **8+ attempts within 30 seconds** from the same source IP (**Rule `100011`**).
4. **Automated Containment:** Triggering **Rule `100011`** invokes Wazuh Active Response (`firewall-drop`), dynamically inserting an `iptables` `DROP` rule on `jeanpubuntu` for **120 seconds** (**Rule `651`**) before automatically restoring network access.

